**Theorem 14.2 (two semidefinite remainders).** Under Assumption 10.1,
if $R_-\succeq0$ and $R_+\succeq0$, then the complete contact
partition has at most five groups. Every multiplier-optimal law
has an exactly data-preserving replacement with at most five low
labels, and $U_5=[r_-,r_+]$. No nonvanishing hypothesis on $L_o$
is needed.

Proof. The nonpositive-height contacts merge by Theorem 13.1.
For each positive-height saturated contact,
$0=D_cF_{0,\epsilon,+}+N_cx^TR_+x$ has two nonnegative
summands. It therefore forces a pure contact with $\ell\ge0$,
in the same compatible group. All saturated contacts now share
pure groups, of which Theorem 12.2 gives at most five. The group
replacement and Theorems 4.2 and 5.2 apply exactly as in Theorem
14.1, regardless of whether $L_o$ vanishes. $\square$

